 {:name "descend-an-even-analytic-function-through-the-squaring-map"
  :lane :solve-lane
  :level :construction
  :status :draft
  :body
  {:problem-class
   "Construct an analytic factor `g` for an even analytic function `h`, with `h z = g (z²)`, across the branch point at zero."
   :construction
   ["Choose a square root only to define the pointwise factor; the selector itself need not be analytic because evenness makes the value independent of the sign."
    "At zero, expand `h` as a formal power series, use vanishing of all odd coefficients, and reindex the even coefficients into a new series."
    "Prove the reindexed series has positive radius using coefficient bounds inherited from the original series."
    "Use `tsum_even_add_odd` and the zero odd part to identify the new series after composition with `z ↦ z²`."
    "At a nonzero point, the derivative of the squaring map is nonzero; use `analyticAt_comp_iff_of_deriv_ne_zero` to descend analyticity through its local inverse."
    "Combine the Taylor-germ argument at the branch point with the inverse-function argument away from it."]
   :key-api
   ["FormalMultilinearSeries.ofScalars"
    "FormalMultilinearSeries.le_mul_pow_of_radius_pos"
    "FormalMultilinearSeries.le_radius_of_bound"
    "HasFPowerSeriesAt.eventually_hasSum"
    "tsum_even_add_odd"
    "analyticAt_comp_iff_of_deriv_ne_zero"]
   :witness
   {:declaration "apm_a96J10_even_factors"
    :helpers
    ["evenFactorRoot"
     "evenFactorRoot_sq"
     "evenFactorRoot_tendsto"
     "evenCoefficients_radius_pos"
     "evenCoefficients_sum"]
    :statement-integrity :intact
    :axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"]
    :sorryAx false}
   :rule
   "For descent through a branched map, split the proof at the branch locus: reindex the invariant Taylor germ where the derivative vanishes, and use the analytic inverse-function theorem everywhere else."
   :boundary
   "Evenness is load-bearing for independence from the two square-root choices and for annihilating odd Taylor coefficients. The inverse-function step cannot cover zero because the derivative of `z ↦ z²` vanishes there."
